[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 395,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 228,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58184,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 228,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibrous hamartoma of infancy is a benign soft tissue neoplasm of infants and young children, showing organoid, triphasic morphology with bundles of bland fibroblastic/myofibroblastic cells; nodules of primitive, rounded or stellate cells with myxoid stroma, and mature adipose tissue.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 396,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 229,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58185,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 229,
        "headingText": "<p>8992/0 Fibrous hamartoma of infancy</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-31T18:52:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 397,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 230,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58186,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 230,
        "headingText": "<p>LC2Y Other specified hamartomata derived from dermal connective tissue</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-23T16:11:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 398,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 231,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58187,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> subdermal fibromatous tumour of infancy.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58188,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58189,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibrous hamartoma of infancy commonly involves the axilla, trunk, upper extremities, and genital regions. This lesion has also been described in other locations, including scalp, foot, hand, orbit, and buttock {28059097; 24525510}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58190,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibrous hamartoma of infancy typically presents as painless solitary subcutaneous masses, sometimes with overlying skin discolouration, oedema, hypertrichosis, and tethering {28059097}. There are rare reports of this tumour type presenting as multiple lesions in the same patient and in patients with tuberous sclerosis and Williams syndrome {28059097; 19319513; 17326745}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58191,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibrous hamartoma of infancy is rare and most often occurs in children aged &lt;&nbsp;2&nbsp;years, with a male predominance. However, it can occur in older children {28059097}. About 15&ndash;25% of cases are congenital {24525510}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58192,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58193,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although fibrous hamartoma of infancy was historically considered a hamartoma, the recent discovery of recurrent <em>EGFR</em> exon&nbsp;20 insertion/duplication mutations in this lesion indicates a neoplastic process {27631514}. Minimal to moderate EGFR (HER1) protein expression has been reported, mostly in the primitive mesenchyme {27631514}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58194,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The excised lesions are poorly circumscribed and of varying size (mean: 3&nbsp;cm). The cut surfaces have a variable amount of adipose tissue and greyish-tan fibrous tissue.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58195,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibrous hamartoma of infancy has a triphasic organoid pattern with haphazardly arranged fascicles of cytologically bland fibroblastic/myofibroblastic cells, admixed with mature adipose tissue and myxoid nodules containing primitive mesenchymal cells with rounded to stellate nuclei. One&nbsp;quarter of cases show areas resembling giant cell fibroblastoma, composed of hyalinized zones containing collagen with cracking artefact forming slit-like spaces lined by flattened tumour cells. Mitotic figures are infrequent, and necrosis is typically absent. Interspersed inflammatory cells can be seen. Very recently, two tumours showing both typical and sarcomatous morphology were reported as part of a larger series {28059097}. By immunohistochemistry, fibrous hamartoma of infancy shows variable expression of SMA in the fibroblastic areas and occasionally in the primitive mesenchyme. CD34 is expressed within the primitive mesenchyme and in areas with giant cell fibroblastoma&ndash;like morphology.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58196,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FNA shows bland-appearing fibroblasts in a collagenous matrix and mature fat without substantial nuclear atypia or mitoses {18499982}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58197,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58198,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> organoid, triphasic morphology; variable prominence of bundles of bland fibroblastic/myofibroblastic cells, nodules of primitive mesenchyme, and mature adipose tissue.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58199,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58200,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most patients are cured with simple excision, although roughly 15% of cases have been reported to locally recur if incompletely excised. The clinical significance of sarcoma-like foci within fibrous hamartoma of infancy is unclear {28059097}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:15:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]